HIVHXB2CG	GenBank	region	1	9719	.	+	1	ID=K03455;Dbxref=taxon:11676;Name=K03455;Note=Human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HXB2)%2C complete genome%3B HIV1/HTLV-III/LAV reference genome.,On Mar 25,1997 this sequence version replaced gi:327742. [6] sites,tat mRNA and other transcript boundaries. [7] sites,tat mRNA.  [8] sites,mRNA splice sites.  [9] sites,27K antigen cds.  [5] sites,gp160 and gp120 coding sequences.  [1] sites,regulatory sequences in the LTR.  [(in) Weiss,R.,Teich,N.,Varmus,H. and Coffin,J. (Eds.),RNA Tumor  Viruses,Secon] review,bases 1 to 9718.  [15] sites,trans-activator function and TAR sequence. [19]  sites,pol coding sequence.  [22] sites,23K sor gene product.  [23] sites,pol NH2-terminal region.  [20] sites,sor 23K protein.  [21] sites,sor 23K protein.  [24] sites,Sp1 binding sites in the promoter region. [17] sites,acceptor and donor splice sites for tat and 27K. [10] sites,deletion mutants in the tat gene.  [18] sites,env gene conserved/varable regions,separate entries.  [16] sites,trs cds boundaries.  [12] sites,trs cds boundaries.  [11] sites,env gene conserved/variable regions,separate entries.  [26] sites,tar or transactivator target.  [13] sites,3' orf mutations.  [14] sites,pol p34 terminus.  [31] sites,promoter,TAR,tat-III mutants.  [32] sites,envelope protein epitopes.  [33] sites,trs/art protein.  [34] sites,inducible enhancer element.  [27] revises [30].  [29] sites,long terminal repeat.  [28] sites,R orf.  [35] sites,sor.  Sequence for [25] kindly provided in computer-readable form by  L.Ratner,19-AUG-1986.  The HXB2 sequence is being used as a reference genome for all the  HIV entries because it has been derived from a demonstrably  infectious clone. Hence not all of the 'sites' references above  were concerned with this isolate. ,HTLV-III/LAV;comment1=On Mar 25%2C 1997 this sequence version replaced gi:327742. [6] sites%3B tat mRNA and other transcript boundaries. [7] sites%3B  tat mRNA.  [8] sites%3B mRNA splice sites.  [9] sites%3B 27K antigen cds.  [5] sites%3B gp160 and gp120 coding sequences.  [1] sites%3B regulatory sequences in the LTR.  [(in) Weiss%2CR.%2C Teich%2CN.%2C Varmus%2CH. and Coffin%2CJ. (Eds.)%3BRNA Tumor  Viruses%2C Secon] review%3B bases 1 to 9718.  [15] sites%3B trans-activator function and TAR sequence. [19]  sites%3B pol coding sequence.  [22] sites%3B 23K sor gene product.  [23] sites%3B pol NH2-terminal region.  [20] sites%3B sor 23K protein.  [21] sites%3B sor 23K protein.  [24] sites%3B Sp1 binding sites in the promoter region. [17] sites%3B  acceptor and donor splice sites for tat and 27K. [10] sites%3B  deletion mutants in the tat gene.  [18] sites%3B env gene conserved/varable regions%3B separate entries.  [16] sites%3B trs cds boundaries.  [12] sites%3B trs cds boundaries.  [11] sites%3B env gene conserved/variable regions%3B separate entries.  [26] sites%3B tar or transactivator target.  [13] sites%3B 3' orf mutations.  [14] sites%3B pol p34 terminus.  [31] sites%3B promoter%2C TAR%2C tat-III mutants.  [32] sites%3B envelope protein epitopes.  [33] sites%3B trs/art protein.  [34] sites%3B inducible enhancer element.  [27] revises [30].  [29] sites%3B long terminal repeat.  [28] sites%3B R orf.  [35] sites%3B sor.  Sequence for [25] kindly provided in computer-readable form by  L.Ratner%2C 19-AUG-1986.  The HXB2 sequence is being used as a reference genome for all the  HIV entries because it has been derived from a demonstrably  infectious clone. Hence not all of the 'sites' references above  were concerned with this isolate. ;date=21-OCT-2002;isolate=HXB2;mol_type=genomic RNA;organism=Human immunodeficiency virus 1;proviral=_no_value
